,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":530.535632083,"data":"--- Problem tech_func_eq [algebra.functional_equations.functional_equations] ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":530.53563333,"data":"Triggers: find all functions f:R-\u003eR such that, functional equation with f composed multiple times, Cauchy-type equation, f(x+y) = f(x)+f(y) or variants\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":530.535634992,"data":"Technique: FE workflow: (1) Guess solution (try f=0,x,-x,kx,kx+c,x^2). (2) Substitute x=y=0 to find f(0). (3) One variable=0 to simplify. (4) Force cancellation by setting arguments equal. (5) Swap x,y for new equation. (6) Replace x with f(t) if bijective. Reduce to Cauchy form when possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":530.535637383,"data":"Question: Find all f:R-\u003eR with f(x+y)+f(x-y) = 2f(x)+2f(y) for all x,y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":530.535638705,"data":"Answer: x=y=0: f(0)+f(0)=4f(0), so f(0)=0. y=x: f(2x)+f(0)=2f(x)+2f(x), so f(2x)=4f(x). This suggests f(x)=cx^2. Verify: c(x+y)^2+c(x-y)^2 = c(x^2+2xy+y^2+x^2-2xy+y^2) = 2cx^2+2cy^2 = 2f(x)+2f(y). For uniqueness: set g(x)=f(x)/f(1), then g(1)=1, g is continuous (if assumed), and g(r)=r^2 for all rationals by induction. With continuity, f(x)=cx^2. Without continuity, pathological solutions exist.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":530.535640387,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":530.535641449,"data":"--- Problem 06788e [algebra.sequences.linear_recurrence] ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":530.535642975,"data":"Triggers: a_{n+1}=f(n)-k*a_n, monotonicity constraint, closed form threshold\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":530.535644316,"data":"Technique: Linear recurrence a_{n+1}=2^n-7a_n with monotonicity constraint. Find minimal 1/a_0 such that a_{n+1}\u003ea_n for all positive n. The recurrence has closed form; the constraint becomes an inequality on a_0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":530.535645773,"data":"Question: A sequence of real numbers {a_n} satisfies that: a_{n+1}=2^n-7a_n, n=0,1,2,... Find the minimal possible value of 1/a_0 such that a_{n+1}\u003ea_n for any positive integer n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":530.535647172,"data":"Answer: Solve the linear recurrence a_{n+1}=2^n-7a_n. The homogeneous solution is C*(-7)^n. The particular solution involves 2^n terms. Express a_n in terms of a_0, then impose a_{n+1}\u003ea_n for all n\u003e=1. This gives a threshold for a_0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":530.535650572,"data":"\n"}
